WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.162
What does each category ask of you?

2
00:00:02.162 --> 00:00:06.424
The data platform book's chapter,
Reading the Audit, takes them in turn.

3
00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:10.697
For a Category 1 instance, nothing in
the infrastructure remains to be built:

4
00:00:10.697 --> 00:00:14.562
the audit has done its job when it
delivers a precise login inventory.

5
00:00:14.562 --> 00:00:18.402
For Category 2, the Arc deployment
is a server-level action,

6
00:00:18.402 --> 00:00:20.162
not a SQL Server change.

7
00:00:20.162 --> 00:00:21.930
The agent connects the server,

8
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:26.101
and Arc installs the Azure extension
for SQL Server automatically.

9
00:00:26.101 --> 00:00:29.453
Once Entra authentication is
configured on the instance,

10
00:00:29.453 --> 00:00:32.038
the next audit re-classifies
it to Category 1.

11
00:00:32.038 --> 00:00:38.073
A Category 3 instance has two paths: upgrade
the engine to SQL Server 2022 or later

12
00:00:38.073 --> 00:00:43.763
and follow the Category 2 path, or document
a formal exception with a sunset date.

13
00:00:43.763 --> 00:00:46.447
A failover cluster
instance re-platforms to a

14
00:00:46.447 --> 00:00:49.201
standalone instance or
an availability group.

15
00:00:49.201 --> 00:00:54.437
And mind the program's N T
L M backstop, April 1, 2027.

16
00:00:54.437 --> 00:00:58.642
A Category 3 instance's remaining
N T L M connections fail then,

17
00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:00.538
whether it's excepted or not.

18
00:01:00.538 --> 00:01:02.540
Its Kerberos connections don't.
